// Histogram of wakeup latency (us) for a specific PID: from a wake request
// to the woken thread being switched in. Recorded for the wakeups the
// target process makes (one of its threads handing a lock to another, say)
// and for wakeups of its main thread. The interval includes the wakeup's
// trip to the woken thread's CPU and any wait for that CPU; under a lock it
// is not the time a thread waited for the lock.
// {{PID}} is replaced by the BpfRunner before execution.
//
// sched_waking runs in the waking thread; sched_wakeup can run on the
// woken thread's CPU, outside the process that asked for the wakeup.
// args->pid (the woken thread) and args->next_pid (the thread switched in)
// are the kernel's thread ids, as the host's PID namespace numbers them;
// pid and tid are bpftrace's. The trace ends with the process's main
// thread, whose thread id is the process id.
//
// Built entirely on sched-family tracepoints, which every kernel ships --
// including vendor kernels (e.g. NVIDIA L4T) that compile out kprobes and
// the syscalls tracepoint family. No BEGIN/END on purpose: maps auto-print
// at exit, and stripped distro bpftrace builds cannot run BEGIN/END
// trigger uprobes.

tracepoint:sched:sched_waking /args->pid == {{PID}} || pid == {{PID}}/ {
  @woken[args->pid] = nsecs;
}

tracepoint:sched:sched_switch /@woken[args->next_pid]/ {
  @wakeup_latency_us = hist((nsecs - @woken[args->next_pid]) / 1000);
  delete(@woken[args->next_pid]);
}

tracepoint:sched:sched_process_exit /tid == {{PID}}/ {
  exit();
}
